3. Purpose of STP
  - This topic covers the causes of loops in a Layer 2 network and briefly explains how spanning tree protocol works. 
  - Redundancy is an important part of the hierarchical design for eliminating single points of failure and preventing disruption of network services to users(sediakan banyak jalan). 
  - Redundant networks require the addition of physical paths, but logical redundancy must also be part of the design. 
  - Having alternate physical paths for data to traverse the network makes it possible for users to access network resources, despite path disruption. 
  - However, redundant paths in a switched Ethernet network may cause both physical and logical Layer 2 loops.
  - Ethernet LANs require a loop-free topology with a single path between any two devices. 
  - A loop in an Ethernet LAN can cause continued propagation of Ethernet frames until a link is disrupted and breaks the loop.

4. Spanning Tree Protocol
  - Spanning Tree Protocol (STP) is a loop-prevention network protocol that allows for redundancy while creating a loop-free Layer 2 topology. 
  - IEEE 802.1D is the original IEEE MAC Bridging standard for STP.
  - STP ni ialah peraturan polis trafik yang memastikan jalan raya kita ada "PLAN B" (jalan simpanan kalau jalan utama rosak), tapi pada masa yang sama tak bagi kereta berpusing-pusing dalam bulatan (loop-free).   
  - Macam mana dia buat: Dia bagi kita simpan banyak kabel (redundancy), tapi dia "tidurkan" satu kabel sementara waktu. 
  - Jadi secara fizikal jalan tu bersambung bulat, tapi secara logik (laluan kereta) ia cuma satu laluan lurus macam dahan pokok (Spanning Tree).
  - Loop-prevention protocol: Peraturan untuk halang data daripada berpusing-pusing tanpa henti.
  - Loop-free Layer 2 topology: Susunan laluan data yang bersih dan lurus (macam dahan pokok), tiada pusingan bulat.
  - IEEE 802.1D: Kod buku peraturan asal (versi pertama) untuk STP yang semua jenama switch wajib ikut.

5. STP Recalculation
  - The trunk link Trunk1 between S2 and S1 has failed.
  - Link between S2 and S3 blocked. S2 unblocks the port for Trunk2.
  - PC1 sends a broadcast frame to S2.
  - S2 forwards the broadcast out all switch ports, except the originationg port and the failed link for Trunk1.
  - S3 forwards the broadcast out all available switch ports, except the originating port.
  - S1 forwards the broadcast only out of F0/3.

6. Issues with Redundant Switch Links
  - Kenapa network boleh crash teruk kalau ada banyak jalan (redundancy) tapi kita lupa hidupkan STP.
  - Apa Jadi Bila Layer 2 Loop Berlaku? Kalau ada jalan bulat dan tiada STP, kereta mainan (data/frames) akan berpusing tanpa henti dan menyebabkan 3 masalah besar:   
    a. MAC Address Table Instability (Polis Trafik Pening): Bayangkan polis trafik ada buku nota untuk catat "Kereta Ali datang dari Pintu A". Tiba-tiba kereta yang sama berpusing dan muncul dari Pintu B pula! Polis trafik asyik padam dan tulis semula buku notanya beribu-ribu kali sesaat sampai dia pening.
    b. Link Saturation (Jalan Raya Jem Teruk): Kereta yang berpusing-pusing tu makin lama makin banyak sehingga memenuhi 100% ruang jalan raya (bandwidth). Kereta lain yang betul-betul nak hantar barang langsung tak boleh lalu.
    c. High CPU Utilization (Otak Switch Terbakar): Sebab sibuk layan kereta berpusing dan tukar-tukar buku nota tanpa henti, otak Switch dan komputer pengguna (CPU) bekerja terlalu keras sampai hang dan network terus tak boleh guna (unusable).
  - Kenapa Layer 2 Lagi Bahaya Dari Layer 3? Compare Layer 3 (Router / IPv4 & IPv6) dengan Layer 2 (Switch / Ethernet):
    a. Layer 3 (Ada Tarikh Luput): Setiap kali paket data lalu dekat Router, Router akan tolak 1 nyawa pada surat tu. Dalam IPv4 nyawa ini dipanggil TTL (Time to Live), manakala dalam IPv6 ia dipanggil Hop Limit. Bila nyawa jadi 0, Router terus buang surat tu ke dalam tong sampah. Jadi ia takkan berpusing selamanya.
    b. Layer 2 (Tiada Tarikh Luput): Switch (Ethernet) tiada fungsi nyawa macam TTL atau Hop Limit. Jadi kalau data dah masuk bulatan di Layer 2, ia akan hidup dan berpusing selama-lamanya tanpa mati-mati sampai ada orang cabut kabel! Sebab itulah STP dicipta khas untuk selamatkan Layer 2.

7. Layer 2 Loops
  - Explain 3 jenis frames yang boleh buat hal kalau STP tak dihidupkan
    a. Broadcast Frames (Pengumuman Pembesar Suara): Contoh ARP Request (macam jerit guna megafon: "Siapa pemilik IP 192.168.1.5?!"). Bila Switch dengar jeritan ni, dia wajib salin dan hantar ke semua pintu (ports) kecuali pintu tempat jeritan tu datang (ingress port). Bila ada laluan bulat, jeritan ni melantun dan berganda tanpa henti!
    b. Multicast Frames (Pengumuman Kumpulan Tertentu): Sama macam Broadcast, kalau Switch tak tahu siapa ahli kumpulan tu, dia akan hantar ke semua pintu juga dan berpusing dalam bulatan.
    c. Unknown Unicast Frames (Surat Tak Jumpa Alamat dalam Buku): Bayangkan posmen (Switch) dapat surat untuk "Rumah Ali", tapi dia tengok dalam buku nota (MAC Address Table) nama Ali belum tercatat lagi. Sebab tak tahu rumah Ali kat pintu mana, posmen terpaksa fotostat surat tu dan hantar ke semua pintu (kecuali pintu asal). Akibatnya, Ali boleh terima surat pendua (duplicate frames) berkali-kali!
  - Macam mana satu network boleh tumbang/bring down dalam beberapa saat sahaja
    a. Broadcast berpusing masuk ikut Pintu 1, keluar Pintu 2, pusing balik masuk Pintu 1.
    b. MAC Database Instability: Switch keliru sebab nampak pengirim yang sama kejap muncul dari Pintu 1, kejap muncul dari Pintu 2. Buku nota MAC Address Table bertukar-tukar tanpa henti.
    c. High CPU Utilization: Otak Switch terlalu penat kemas kini buku nota dan fotostat beribu-ribu frame setiap saat.
    d. Network Down: Switch terus "pengsan" (tak mampu hantar data lain lagi) hanya dalam masa beberapa saat!

8. Broadcast Storm
  - A broadcast storm is an abnormally high number of broadcasts overwhelming the network during a specific amount of time. 
  - Broadcast storms can disable a network within seconds by overwhelming switches and end devices. 
  - Broadcast storms can be caused by a hardware problem such as a faulty NIC or from a Layer 2 loop in the network.
  - Layer 2 broadcasts in a network, such as ARP Requests are very common. 
  - A Layer 2 loop is likely to have immediate and disabling consequences on the network. 
  - Layer 2 multicasts are typically forwarded the same way as a broadcast by the switch. 
  - So, although IPv6 packets are never forwarded as a Layer 2 broadcast, ICMPv6 Neighbor Discovery uses Layer 2 multicasts.
  - A host caught in a Layer 2 loop is not accessible to other hosts on the network.
  - Due to the constant changes in its MAC address table, the switch does not know out of which port to forward unicast frames. 
  - In the previous animation, the switches will have the incorrect ports listed for PC1. 
  - Any unknown unicast frame destined for PC1 loops around the network, just as the broadcast frames do. 
  - More and more frames looping around the network eventually creates a broadcast storm.
  - To prevent these issues from occurring in a redundant network, some type of spanning tree must be enabled on the switches. 
  - Spanning tree is enabled, by default, on Cisco switches to prevent Layer 2 loops from occurring.

9. The Spanning Tree Algorithm
  - Radia Perlman: Her spanning tree algorithm (STA) creates a loop-free topology by selecting a single root bridge where all other switches determine a single least-cost path.
  - The spanning tree algoritm begins by selecting a single root bridge.
  - Each switch will determine a single, least cost path from itself to the root bridge.
  - STP prevents loops from occuring by configuring a loop-free path through the network using strategically placed "blocking-state" ports.
  - The switches running STP are able to compensate for failures by dynamically unblocking the previously blocked ports and permitting traffic to traverse the alternate paths.
  - STP ensures that there is only one logical path between all destinations on the network by intentionally blocking redundant paths that could cause a loop. 
  - When a port is blocked, user data is prevented from entering or leaving that port. 
  - Blocking the redundant paths is critical to preventing loops on the network.
  - A blocked port has the effect of making that link a non-forwarding link between the two switches.
  - This creates a topology where each switch has ony a single path to the root bridge, similar to branches on a tree that connect to the root of the tree.
  - Link Failure Causes Recalculation: The physical paths still exist to provide redundancy, but these paths are disabled to prevent the loops from occuring.
  - If the path is ever needed to compensate for a network cable or switch failure, STP recalculates the paths and unblocks the necessarry ports to allow the redundant path to become active.
  - STP recalculations can occur at any time a new switch or new inter-switch link is added to the network.
  - STP prevents loops from occurring by configuring a loop-free path through the network using strategically placed "blocking-state" ports.
  - The switches running STP are able to compensate for failure by dynamically unblocking the previously blocked ports and permitting traffic to traverse to alternate paths.
